course: dictionary
lesson: 14254
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words change their meaning depending on where you are. Nervy is one of them. In Britain, Australia and New Zealand, nervy means nervous, jumpy and easily worried. «Mia felt nervy before her spelling test.» Mia is not brave that morning. She is worried.
explain: In American informal speech, nervy means bold and cheeky, daring to do things others would not. «The nervy kid asked the teacher for a second slice of cake.» The context shows the kid is cheeky, not scared. Read the clues around the word to find which meaning fits.
explain: Nervy can also describe a tense moment, such as «a nervy finish» to a football match, where the game is close and nobody can relax. Ask yourself: what word would fit here? Then put your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
fact: Nervy means nervous, jumpy and easily worried in British, Australian and New Zealand English.
fact: In American informal English, nervy means bold and cheeky, daring to do things others would not.
fact: «A nervy horse» in a British story is a jumpy horse. «A nervy kid» in an American story is a cheeky or brave kid.
fact: The same word can mean different things in different countries, so check where the story is set.
fact: The comparison forms are nervier and nerviest, as in «the nerviest finish of the season».
exercise: «Before the test, Sam felt nervy and kept biting his pen.» What does nervy mean here? | Happy and calm | *Nervous and worried | Very tired | Bold and daring
exercise: In an American story, a girl laughs, «Ha! That was a nervy move, Sam!» after Sam steals a biscuit. What does nervy mean? | *Bold and cheeky | Jumpy and scared | Wet and cold | Lost and confused
exercise: «It was a nervy finish, with only one goal in it.» What does nervy suggest? | Boring and slow | Cold and wet | Funny and silly | *Tense and close
test: «The new puppy was nervy around loud noises.» Which word could replace nervy? | Sleepy | Hungry | Bold | *Jumpy
test: In a US story, «Lily's nervy plan was to climb the school fence.» What does nervy mean? | Sad and quiet | Wet and cold | *Bold and daring | Angry and loud
exam: In a British story, «the nervy child hid behind her mum.» What does nervy mean? | Bold and cheeky | *Nervous and easily frightened | Tall and thin | Loud and happy
